# Bambu print projects (bambu3mf)

This skill writes Bambu Studio project files (.3mf) that open in Bambu Studio
with the print's own tuned profiles already selected. It runs offline: the tool
(pure Python 3.9+, no installs) and Bambu Lab's official system profiles
({{PROFILE_INFO}}) are bundled in this folder.

## Running it
`SKILL_DIR` is the folder containing this SKILL.md. Every command:

```bash
python3 "$SKILL_DIR/run.py" <command> [options]
```

Work in a writable folder (e.g. `mkdir -p /tmp/work && cd /tmp/work`). Files
the user uploaded are usually in `/mnt/user-data/uploads/`. Save finished
`.3mf`/`.json` files to `/mnt/user-data/outputs/` (if that folder exists) so
the user can download them.

Commands: `printers`, `presets --printer P [--type process|filament] [--filter TEXT]`,
`search "<words>" --printer P` (setting keys, allowed values, current value),
`show "<preset>" --keys k1,k2`, `build spec.json -o out.3mf [--dry-run]`,
`inspect file.3mf [--values]`, `edit file.3mf -o new.3mf --set k=v [--filament N | --object NAME | --preset "<name>" | --all-variants]`,
`validate file.3mf`, `extract-presets file.3mf -o dir`, `export-preset ...`, `mesh file.stl`.

## Owner's setup
{{SETUP}}

## Workflow
1. Clarify only what changes the result: printer and nozzle (if not set above),
   material(s), and the goal (strength, looks, speed, fit, heat or outdoor use).
   Pick sensible defaults for the rest and say so.
2. Check the model: `mesh <file>` (size, triangle count, inside-out). Choose
   the orientation (`rotate`): flat face down, strength along the layers,
   fewest supports.
3. Choose presets: the process by layer height for the goal (0.12–0.16 for
   detail, 0.20 standard, 0.24–0.28 drafts); filaments with
   `presets --printer P --type filament --filter <material>`. Use `search` for
   any key you aren't sure about, and read `references/TUNING.md`.
4. Write the spec (`references/SPEC.md`, examples in `examples/`):
   - `"profile": "<print name>"`: the file embeds its own named profiles.
   - Unless the user wants a single material, add `variants` for plausible
     materials (PLA + PETG; ASA/ABS for heat or outdoor use when the printer
     has presets). Shared tuning goes in top-level `settings`, material tuning
     in each variant.
5. `build spec.json -o /tmp/work/<name>.3mf --dry-run`, check it, then build
   for real. The output must contain `"validation": {"ok": true}`. Fix errors
   and address warnings.
6. Copy the .3mf to the outputs folder. Tell the user:
   - the embedded profiles and variants, and how to switch: select
     "<print> - PETG" in the filament slot **and** the process dropdown;
   - each changed setting with a one-line reason;
   - what to check in Bambu Studio (orientation, supports).

To modify an uploaded project: `inspect` → `edit` → `validate`.

## Rules
- Never edit a 3MF's internal files by hand; always use `build`/`edit`.
  Bambu Studio silently reverts overrides that aren't recorded properly, and
  the tool handles that bookkeeping.
- Change as few settings as possible; prefer picking the right preset over
  overriding many keys.
- Don't touch printer or filament G-code unless asked.
- Per-object, modifier and height-range overrides take process settings only.
- Positions are bed coordinates of the footprint centre; omit `position` to
  auto-arrange.
- The tool doesn't slice. Never state print times or filament use; Bambu
  Studio computes those.
